ect evidence, his other strengths (conscientiousness, stress handling, technical competence) still make him the best, and we can mitigate the unknowns with training and monitoring. Or we could suggest we need more info before final decision, perhaps consider other candidates. But the instruction says we should not delay decision beyond available info; we must make a recommendation based on current evidence. We can argue that the lack of evidence is a gap but not a disqualifier; we can still recommend B, but note that we should monitor those aspects.\n\nWe should reference the discussion: Agent 1 and 3 have confirmed lack of data on D's crew cooperation, and Agent 1 says no data on B's decision quality etc. So we can say: While we have no direct evidence on B's decision quality, attention accuracy, or adaptability, his proven reliability, stress resilience, and technical competence still outweigh the unknowns, especially compared to other candidates who have clear deficits (C's communication, D's interpersonal issues, A's lack of stress data and organizational issues). Therefore I keep my vote for B.\n\nWe need to include explicit facts: B very conscientious, handles stress well, good at assessing weather, excellent computer skills; B pretentious, wrong tone, grumpy, uncooperative. Also mention that lack of data on decision quality etc.\n\nThus produce public message and metadata JSON with vote Candidate B."}
